*Key points*: The daemon opens `/sys/fs/bpf/<map>/events` where `<map>` is derived from the map name (`"dropped"`). It enables all CPUs (`perfmon__enable`) so every per‑CPU buffer becomes readable via `read()`. Each read returns a binary blob containing one or more headers; we print them as hexadecimal strings for visibility.

## Verifier Constraints & Safety Notes
- **No loops**: The program contains only straight‑line code; this satisfies the verifier’s requirement for bounded execution time.
- **Stack usage**: Only a few local variables (`cpu`, `hdr`) are allocated on the stack – well within the verifier’s limit of 512 bytes.
- **Helper call limits**: Each invocation of `bpf_*` helpers must be followed by a null check on their return value; we do this implicitly by returning early on failure.
- **Map size**: Because we use a single entry (`max=1`), there is no need for additional synchronization primitives; each CPU writes independently into its own buffer.
- **Security**: The daemon runs with normal privileges but can be restricted via Linux capabilities (`CAP\_SYS\_ADMIN`) or SELinux policies to limit which users may read `/sys/fs/bpf/*`.

## Common Pitfalls & Mitigations
| Pitfall | Symptom | Mitigation |
|---------|---------|------------|
| Map not found at runtime | `ENOENT` when opening `/sys/fs/bpf/<map>/events` | Ensure the BPF object containing this map is loaded before starting the daemon; use proper ordering in startup scripts |
| Permissions denied on `/sys/fs/bpf/*` | Daemon cannot open file descriptor | Run daemon as root or grant appropriate capabilities via file ACLs |
| Header size mismatch | Userspace prints garbage or crashes | Verify that `sizeof(*hdr)` matches exactly what you expect; avoid adding padding fields unless you adjust both sides |
| High packet rate causing backlog | Kernel drops packets before they reach userspace | Increase perf buffer size via sysctl (`kernel.perf_*`) or filter at XDP level |
| Missing libbpf support on older kernels | Compilation errors | Use libbpf version ≥ 0.7 which provides stable wrappers around these helpers |

